Water rendering and composition performance
==========================================

Scope and contracts
  Remove recurring executable preparation and unnecessary water shader/background work.
  Preserve the existing water-volume model, HDR transport, refracted scattering direction,
  mandatory bilateral receiver filtering, and precompiled UV/x2/x4/x8 quality options.
  Preserve full/half background resolution, opaque receiver validity, OIT ordering, and the
  verified shallow-water banding/sparkle fixes. Performance changes must not restore confidence
  fading, unsupported receiver hits, foreground leakage, or disappearing distortion.
  Reuse shader registry, resource lifetime, pipeline descriptions, StateCache, and profiling owners.
  Do not introduce a new shader-cache or profiling framework for this work.
  Full-scene HDR and replacement of the base-game sky are separate work, not prerequisites.

References
  - docs/PBR.WaterRefraction.md: receiver, traversal, quality and composition contracts.
  - docs/PBR.WaterMedium.md: signed boundary accumulation and physical transport.
  - docs/PBR.Liquids.md: liquid/OIT integration.
  - artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/findings.md: original capture evidence and measurement limitations.
  - docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.todo: broader state ownership migration.

Evidence and completed work
  ☑ Retain separate volume and surface LiquidShaderProgram owners.
    - pbr_water_volume retains mode 3; pbr_liquid retains surface mode 0.
    - Removed per-frame mode changes from WaterVolumeRenderer. Existing registry reload and
      disposal own both executables and their independent inputs.
    - Isolated build and 20/20 focused GPU tests passed, including stable IDs during alternating
      use, surface-only quality changes, reload and disposal. See owner-fix-tests.log/.trx in
      artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis. In-game improvement has not yet been measured.
  Original capture: frame 1367 at 3440x1377, before the owner fix. It records two liquid
  program creations/links and approximately 10.45 ms/14.51 ms preparation intervals.
  Those are captured CPU intervals, not GPU pass times or uncaptured gameplay costs.
  Volume/surface fragment counts were approximately 1.95M/1.85M; extreme water overdraw was
  not demonstrated in that view. Generic replay GPU timings were unreliable. The captured
  surface binary matched a Debug artifact whose receipt specifies -O0.

Measurement policy
  Use the same scene, camera, viewport, settings, loaded build and shader optimization policy
  for before/after comparisons. Record CPU preparation separately from GPU draws and frame time.
  Reuse existing timer scopes, adding narrow scopes only where the affected work is unmeasured.
  Do not infer production GPU cost from headless test duration, SPIR-V size, or texture-fetch bounds.
  User-run captures provide live acceptance; do not launch or control the game automatically.
  Missing live measurements must remain explicitly unmeasured, not be replaced by estimates.
  Keep checks proportional to each change; do not require an exhaustive visual matrix for every
  independent optimization. Final targeted comparisons may reuse earlier applicable evidence.

Phase 1 — Remove composite executable replacement
  ☐ Retain independently prepared pre-overlay and ordinary composite owners.
    - Audit actual capture/ordinary callers: capture forces LumOn off; ordinary composition can
      enable it. Both currently mutate one pbr_composite executable through structural options.
    - Keep the capture environment fallback and ordinary LumOn inputs unchanged. Configure
      each owner at its appropriate settings/reload boundary, without per-frame option toggling.
    - Update PrepareFrame consistently, but do not activate deferred full-scene HDR coordination.
    - Preserve frame inputs, binding contracts, registry lifecycle and shared offline binaries.
  ☐ Verify LumOn on/off, refraction enabled/disabled, and alternating capture/ordinary use.
    - Check retained program IDs and absence of repeated asset reads/preparation after warmup.
    - Verify quality/settings changes, reload and disposal without stale inputs or resource leaks.
  Completion: both required composite executables remain resident; alternating passes do not
  create/load/link/delete programs or rebuild their interfaces. Record any live timing separately.

Phase 2 — Supply inverse projection once per frame
  ☐ Add inverse projection to the existing liquid frame binding/UBO contract and frame capture.
    - Reuse the owning matrix convention and existing CPU inversion APIs.
    - Share it across ray tracing, UV distortion and fallback; remove fragment matrix inversions.
    - Update production-importing fixtures and generated binding expectations together.
  ☐ Verify reconstructed positions and refraction across camera views and full/half backgrounds.
    - Confirm compiled production variants no longer contain the redundant matrix inversions.
  Completion: existing rendering behavior is preserved and inverse projection is supplied through
  the normal frame input owner, including diagnostic paths that import the shared algorithms.

Phase 3 — Separate receiver geometry search from radiance reconstruction
  ☐ Audit the receiver validity contract before deferring color samples.
    - Color alpha currently carries receiver validity; preserve that information during search.
    - Reuse existing depth/source metadata where valid. If a representation change is needed,
      document its producer/consumer contract and costs before implementing it.
  ☐ Avoid loading/interpolating radiance for intermediate receiver probes where validity permits.
    - Retain required depth taps, bilateral layer rejection, source positions, precision bounds,
      slope/plane validation and supported-triangle tests.
    - Reconstruct accepted receiver radiance with the same spatial weights and supported coverage.
    - Preserve UV fallback and full/half source-coordinate semantics.
  ☐ Verify invalid color/depth coverage, foreground occluders, shallow shores, steep walls and edges.
    - Compare actual sample work and rendering against the prior implementation using focused cases.
  Completion: intermediate radiance work is reduced without weakening receiver validation or
  changing accepted shading. Record bounds separately from measured GPU savings.

Phase 4 — Reuse the trace seed in UV fallback
  ☐ Pass the already evaluated interface seed into fallback through the shared receiver contract.
    - Preserve valid and invalid seed outcomes; do not reuse the last marched receiver as the seed.
    - Avoid copying an unnecessarily large payload or extending its lifetime without checking cost.
    - Keep UV-only quality independent and retain total-internal-reflection/early-out behavior.
    - Retain x2/x4/x8 traversal ceilings and separately account for fallback sampling.
  ☐ Verify exhausted traces, invalid initial support, shallow x2 transitions and screen edges.
    - Demonstrate removal of duplicate seed sampling without changing fallback radiance/geometry.
  Completion: fallback reuses equivalent initial support and preserves the existing distortion fixes.
  Dependency: settle Phase 3 receiver payload changes before finalizing seed handoff.

Phase 5 — Skip scattering illumination for zero-scattering media
  ☐ Add a bounded zero-scattering path to volume-boundary shading.
    - Skip shadow/phase/source-light evaluation when all effective scattering coefficients are zero.
    - Preserve absorption, signed optical depth, submerged length and boundary-count accumulation.
    - Preserve nonzero scattering and mixed-material boundary behavior; zero density must stay valid.
    - Audit analogous surface calculations, but do not skip lighting shared with reflections.
  ☐ Verify clear water, authored scattering, mixed boundaries and underwater camera transport.
    - Compare GPU cost for coherent clear-water regions and mixed media; branch overhead may differ.
  Completion: zero-scattering output is unchanged and unnecessary source-light work is avoided;
  do not claim a measured speedup without an applicable comparison.

Phase 6 — Capture refraction backgrounds only when needed
  ☐ Identify an existing authoritative water-demand signal available before the hand overlay.
    - Account for underwater views, partially visible water, frustum/screen transitions, resize,
      first-/third-person rendering, and settings changes. Avoid synchronous GPU readback.
    - Prefer conservative extra capture over missing a required current-frame background.
  ☐ Gate pre-overlay lighting/composition using that signal when it proves capture unnecessary.
    - Invalidate skipped publication so stale backgrounds cannot be consumed.
    - Retain resources across ordinary visibility changes; retire through existing lifecycle owners.
    - Preserve the hand-occlusion exclusion and environment fallback behavior.
    - If no safe signal exists, record the missing ownership contract and measured benefit before
      expanding scope; do not add an unreliable camera-block-only visibility heuristic.
  ☐ Verify dry views, entering/exiting water views, underwater views and first-person overlays.
  Completion: unnecessary captures are skipped without stale or absent required receivers.

Phase 7 — Review remaining bandwidth and validate the combined result
  ☐ Obtain a matched optimized-build comparison after executable-lifetime fixes.
    - Separate preparation, water-depth, volume-boundary, surface/OIT, pre-overlay capture and
      ordinary composition costs using existing profiling owners where possible.
    - Compare affected quality/resolution selections and at least a water-heavy and dry view;
      retain build/settings details alongside results. No fixed FPS target is assumed.
  ☐ Investigate remaining volume-target and composition bandwidth only if measurements justify it.
    - Two full-resolution RGBA32F volume targets remain a known cost; storage size is not bandwidth.
    - Evaluate coverage restriction or compatible pass/resource reuse before changing precision.
    - Do not blindly switch signed accumulation to RGBA16F, remove required backfaces, or enable
      depth rejection that loses valid entry/exit boundaries. Any such change needs a separate
      documented numerical/visibility justification and focused regression coverage.
    - Keep measured findings with no justified implementation explicitly documented as such.
  ☐ Review focused regressions, linked documentation and user-run visual/performance feedback.
    - Preserve shallow-wall continuity, x2 stability, receiver rejection and underwater transitions.
    - Record measured improvements, remaining bottlenecks and any unmeasured acceptance items.
  Completion: implemented optimizations have applicable correctness evidence and an honest
  performance record; speculative bandwidth redesign is not an automatic completion requirement.
